1
00:00:00.003 --> 00:00:02.773
Welcome to LLMs as Operating Systems:

2
00:00:02.773 --> 00:00:03.807
Agent Memory.

3
00:00:03.807 --> 00:00:05.909
Built in partnership with Letta.

4
00:00:05.909 --> 00:00:09.279
In this course,
you'll explore and build memory for AI agents.

5
00:00:09.613 --> 00:00:14.584
You will also explored the idea that an LLM agent
can manage its own context window,

6
00:00:14.885 --> 00:00:18.522
essentially acting as the operating system
in an AI application.

7
00:00:18.655 --> 00:00:19.356
Your instructors

8
00:00:19.356 --> 00:00:23.059
for this course are two authors of
of the influential paper

9
00:00:23.126 --> 00:00:27.130
MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems
and are experts on this topic.

10
00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:29.833
Welcome, Charles Packer and Sarah Wooders.

11
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:30.734
Thanks for having us.

12
00:00:30.734 --> 00:00:31.601
Thank you.

13
00:00:31.601 --> 00:00:36.339
If you've taken some taken some of our previous courses
on prompting LLMs via an API,

14
00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.744
you might be familiar with the idea
that although LLMs can do amazing things,

15
00:00:41.044 --> 00:00:42.879
they don't have persistent memory

16
00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:45.882
and that you need to manage its memory
explicitly.

17
00:00:46.149 --> 00:00:50.320
Indeed, the common example is
if you make one API call to say "Hello,

18
00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:53.990
my name is Sarah" and then make a separate
API call to ask "What is my name",

19
00:00:54.291 --> 00:00:55.592
it won't remember.

20
00:00:55.592 --> 00:00:57.461
If you want it to remember,
you have to pass

21
00:00:57.461 --> 00:01:00.497
in the conversation's history again
with each additional API call.

22
00:01:00.997 --> 00:01:05.535
By including information, the prompts
also referred to as the input context,

23
00:01:05.802 --> 00:01:06.803
you can give the model

24
00:01:06.803 --> 00:01:10.240
additional information
to actually use to generate is output.

25
00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:11.608
Exactly.

26
00:01:11.608 --> 00:01:15.479
And what is in the context window
really determines the behavior of the LLM

27
00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:16.847
and your application.

28
00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:18.715
For example, a chatbot application

29
00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:22.152
has conversational memory that stores
earlier exchanges in the conversation.

30
00:01:22.652 --> 00:01:25.655
You may also want to track personal facts
or names over time.

31
00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:27.891
Or keep track of tasks.

32
00:01:27.891 --> 00:01:31.061
You may want to share information
between LLMs in different agents.

33
00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:32.362
In RAG

34
00:01:32.362 --> 00:01:36.099
applications, you retrieve relevant
information from an external datasource

35
00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:38.502
and introduce it
into the context of the LLM.

36
00:01:38.502 --> 00:01:41.671
So an LLMs can make use of any information

37
00:01:41.671 --> 00:01:45.008
that is included in its input context
to generate its response.

38
00:01:45.408 --> 00:01:49.145
But the space available in the input
context window is limited,

39
00:01:49.446 --> 00:01:54.417
and using longer input contexts also cost
more and results in slower processing.

40
00:01:54.718 --> 00:01:57.721
So managing this in-context information

41
00:01:57.921 --> 00:02:01.091
what to include in this input
context is really important.

42
00:02:01.558 --> 00:02:04.194
The MemGPT paper describes
a novel approach

43
00:02:04.194 --> 00:02:07.197
to this - to let the LLM manage it.

44
00:02:07.197 --> 00:02:11.368
If you're familiar with the concept
of virtual memory from computer systems,

45
00:02:11.601 --> 00:02:16.406
there's a nice analogy
that MemGPT uses to explain this idea.

46
00:02:17.007 --> 00:02:19.843
But if you aren't familiar
with that concept of virtual memory,

47
00:02:19.843 --> 00:02:20.610
don't worry about it.

48
00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.613
This course will still make sense
and still be useful to you.

49
00:02:23.847 --> 00:02:25.982
But Charles,
why don't you explain that analogy.

50
00:02:25.982 --> 00:02:26.650
Sure.

51
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:30.487
So we like to think of the context window,
like the virtual memory on a computer.

52
00:02:30.687 --> 00:02:33.256
Your computer thinks it has a really large
memory,

53
00:02:33.256 --> 00:02:35.992
much bigger than the physical memory
it actually has.

54
00:02:35.992 --> 00:02:36.927
Specifically,

55
00:02:36.927 --> 00:02:39.329
it has a very large virtual memory.

56
00:02:39.329 --> 00:02:39.930
When it tries

57
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:43.466
to reference a virtual location
that is not present in physical memory,

58
00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:47.637
the operating system will first make room
for it by moving a block of information

59
00:02:47.737 --> 00:02:51.908
that is in physical memory out to disk,
preserving any changes in that block,

60
00:02:52.208 --> 00:02:55.579
and then fetching the new block
of information with a reference address

61
00:02:55.745 --> 00:02:58.315
from disk back into physical memory.

62
00:02:58.315 --> 00:03:00.951
Similarly,
you can think of the context window

63
00:03:00.951 --> 00:03:05.355
of an LLM as analogous
to the physical memory. In our system,

64
00:03:05.355 --> 00:03:08.858
an LLM agent
includes the role of the operating system

65
00:03:08.892 --> 00:03:10.827
and makes decisions about what information

66
00:03:10.827 --> 00:03:12.696
should be included in the context window.

67
00:03:12.696 --> 00:03:16.066
AI agents can use an LLM
for planning, use tools,

68
00:03:16.066 --> 00:03:20.003
and make decisions such as deciding
to stop or continue a task.

69
00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:24.708
A similar approach
also lets an AI agent manage as a memory.

70
00:03:25.141 --> 00:03:26.810
To support memory management,

71
00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.146
the agent is given a section of
the context window for long term memory,

72
00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.349
which the agent can write to.

73
00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:36.486
The agent is given tools to access
external storage, such as databases,

74
00:03:36.686 --> 00:03:39.422
to create a larger memory store

75
00:03:39.422 --> 00:03:43.860
combining tools to write to both its
in context and external memory,

76
00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:45.562
as well as tools to search

77
00:03:45.562 --> 00:03:48.898
external memory and place those results
into the LLM context,

78
00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:52.732
So in this course, you'll
get to put these ideas into practice.

79
00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:53.700
In these lessons,

80
00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.670
you will build an LLM agent
that can edit its own memory from scratch.

81
00:03:58.037 --> 00:04:01.074
This will ground you
in the basic ideas of LLM memory management.

82
00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:05.712
Next, you'll go beyond the basics
and learn the key concepts from the MemGPT paper.

83
00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:09.716
Then we'll introduce Letta an open source
agent framework in which agents

84
00:04:09.716 --> 00:04:12.752
have the tools and information
to manage the context window.

85
00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:17.557
You can use Letta not only to build MemGPT
agents as described in the original paper,

86
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:21.728
but also go beyond the research paper
and build agents with more advanced types

87
00:04:21.728 --> 00:04:22.362
of memory.

88
00:04:22.362 --> 00:04:23.396
In multiple lessons,

89
00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:26.766
you'll create agents and explore
the details of how memory is built.

90
00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:30.470
You'll put this into practice
by building a custom task memory.

91
00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:32.939
You will use this knowledge
in applications,

92
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:35.308
including your own research agent
and an H.R.

93
00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:38.211
multi-agent application,
where agents share a memory.

94
00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:41.881
We want to acknowledge some of the people
who helped create this course.

95
00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:46.286
Professor Joseph Gonzalez,
who is a UC Berkeley

96
00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:50.423
faculty member
and a Letta advisor. The Letta team.

97
00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.259
And from DeepLearning.AI
Geoff Ladwig.

98
00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.762
The ideas here are really exciting.

99
00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:59.399
Letting an AI agent manage
its own memory is a novel

100
00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:02.735
and powerful technique,
and it provides a powerful infrastructure

101
00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.772
on top of which to build
many applications.

102
00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:08.708
Let's go on to the next video
to start learning about this.

